---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../data/tools';

const tool = toolBySlug['time-zone-converter'];
const breadcrumbItems = [{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: tool.title }];
const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "Time Zone Converter: Plan Across Time Zones",
    "description": "Time Zone Converter: Plan Across Time Zones. Free online calculator included.",
    "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "FreeOnlineToolkit" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "FreeOnlineToolkit" },
    "datePublished": "2026-07-26",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-26",
    "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://freeonlinetoolkit.org/time-zone-converter/" }
  }
];
---

<ToolLayout title="Time Zone Converter: Plan Across Time Zones | FreeOnlineToolkit" description="Time Zone Converter: Plan Across Time Zones. Free online calculator included." breadcrumbItems={breadcrumbItems} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <fragment slot="heading"><h1>Time Zone Converter: Plan Across Time Zones</h1></fragment>

  <div class="key-takeaways" style="background:#eff6ff;border:1px solid #93c5fd;border-radius:8px;padding:1.25rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0;color:#1e40af;">Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul style="margin-bottom:0;">
      <li>The world has 38+ time zones (not just 24) because some zones use 30-minute and 45-minute offsets - India (UTC+5:30) and Nepal (UTC+5:45) are examples.</li>
      <li>Daylight Saving Time shifts clocks by one hour in many countries but starts and ends on different dates, creating 4-5 week windows where zone comparisons are unreliable.</li>
      <li>UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) is the global reference standard - specifying times in UTC eliminates ambiguity when coordinating across multiple zones.</li>
      <li>The International Date Line (roughly following 180 degrees longitude) means that when it is Monday morning in Tokyo, it can still be Sunday afternoon in New York.</li>
      <li>Good meeting scheduling practice: always include the time zone abbreviation (EST, PST, CET) or use UTC to avoid confusion.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

    <p>In a globally connected world, time zone confusion is one of the most common sources of missed meetings, late deliveries, and coordination failures. The Earth is divided into roughly 38 time zones (not the neat 24 you might expect) because political boundaries, daylight saving rules, and half-hour offsets create a patchwork that is far more complex than simple longitude-based divisions. India spans a single time zone (UTC+5:30) across a landmass that would naturally cover two, while China uses Beijing time (UTC+8) nationwide even though its western regions are geographically closer to UTC+5.</p>

    <p><strong>Daylight Saving Time</strong> (DST) adds another layer of complexity. In the Northern Hemisphere, clocks spring forward in March and fall back in October, but the exact dates differ between the US (second Sunday of March), Europe (last Sunday of March), and other regions. This creates 4-5 week windows each spring and autumn where the offset between two zones is temporarily different from the rest of the year. Australia complicates things further by having states that observe DST alongside states that do not, all within the same country.</p>

    <p>For <strong>business coordination</strong>, the gold standard is to specify times in UTC and let each participant convert to their local time. This eliminates the ambiguity that arises when someone says "3 PM" without specifying a zone. Many distributed teams use tools that display multiple time zones side by side, and international organisations like the United Nations and airlines universally use UTC (or "Zulu time") for scheduling and documentation. The ISO 8601 format (2026-07-26T15:00:00Z) is the unambiguous way to represent a moment in time.</p>

    <p>Understanding time zone differences also has practical implications for <strong>travel planning</strong>. Jet lag is directly related to the number of time zones crossed - each zone typically requires about one day of adjustment. Business travellers heading east (which is harder on the body clock than heading west) should plan important meetings for later in their trip when possible. And anyone scheduling a call with someone on the other side of the International Date Line needs to remember that when it is Monday morning in Tokyo, it is still Sunday afternoon in New York - a full calendar day difference.</p>

  <fragment slot="intro">
    <p class="tool-intro">Use the free calculator below to get instant results. Read on for a full guide to the topic, key formulas, and expert tips.</p>
  </fragment>

<fragment slot="calculator">
    <div class="calc-card">
      <h2>Time Zone Converter</h2>

      <div class="form-row">
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="tzFrom">From Time Zone</label>
          <select id="tzFrom"></select>
        </div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="tzTo">To Time Zone</label>
          <select id="tzTo"></select>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="form-row">
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="tzDate">Date</label>
          <input type="date" id="tzDate">
        </div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="tzTime">Time</label>
          <input type="time" id="tzTime" value="12:00">
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-group">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" id="convertBtn">Convert</button>
        <button class="btn btn-secondary" id="nowBtn">Now</button>
      </div>

      <div class="result-box" id="tzResult">
        <div class="result-value" id="tzResultValue">--:--</div>
        <div class="result-label" id="tzResultLabel">Converted Time</div>
        <div class="result-details">
          <div><span class="label">Source Time</span><span class="value" id="tzSourceDisplay">-</span></div>
          <div><span class="label">Target Time</span><span class="value" id="tzTargetDisplay">-</span></div>
          <div><span class="label">Time Difference</span><span class="value" id="tzDiff">-</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="content">
    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>What This Calculator Shows</h2>
      <p>This time zone converter supports 27 time zones covering every major global region. Select your source and target time zones, enter a date and time, and click Convert to see the equivalent time in the target zone along with the time difference in hours. The <strong>Now</strong> button auto-fills your current local time for quick conversions.</p>
    </div>

<div class="content-section">
      <h2>How to Use the Time Zone Converter</h2>
      <p>Select your <strong>source time zone</strong> and <strong>target time zone</strong>. Choose a date and time, then click <strong>Convert</strong>. Click <strong>Now</strong> to set the current date and time in your local time zone automatically.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Time Zone Reference</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>UTC / GMT</strong> — Coordinated Universal Time</li>
        <li><strong>EST / EDT</strong> — Eastern Time (New York, Toronto) — UTC-5/UTC-4</li>
        <li><strong>CST / CDT</strong> — Central Time (Chicago, Mexico City) — UTC-6/UTC-5</li>
        <li><strong>PST / PDT</strong> — Pacific Time (Los Angeles, Vancouver) — UTC-8/UTC-7</li>
        <li><strong>CET / CEST</strong> — Central European Time (Paris, Berlin) — UTC+1/UTC+2</li>
        <li><strong>IST</strong> — India Standard Time — UTC+5:30</li>
        <li><strong>JST</strong> — Japan Standard Time (Tokyo, Seoul) — UTC+9</li>
        <li><strong>AEST / AEDT</strong> — Australian Eastern Time — UTC+10/UTC+11</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <FAQ items={[
      { question: "Does this converter handle Daylight Saving Time?", answer: "Yes. The converter uses UTC offsets that reflect standard time zone definitions. However, DST transitions can vary by country, so always double-check around DST changeover dates." },
      { question: "What does UTC mean?", answer: "UTC stands for Coordinated Universal Time, the primary time standard by which the world regulates clocks and time. It is effectively the same as GMT." },
      { question: "How do I schedule a meeting across time zones?", answer: "Enter your local time as the source, then select the participant's time zone as the target. Good practice is to specify times in UTC when coordinating across multiple zones." },
    ]} />

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Next Steps</h2>
      <p>Select your source and target time zones above to start converting. For recurring international meetings, consider establishing a standing UTC time that all participants can reference. And always double-check conversions during the 4-5 week DST transition windows in March and October.</p>
    </div>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="sidebar">
    <RelatedTools currentSlug="time-zone-converter" category="utility" />
  </fragment>
</ToolLayout>


<script>
import { showResult } from '../utils/calculator-utils';

const timeZones = [
  { name: 'UTC / GMT (UTC+0)', offset: 0 },
  { name: 'GMT (Western Europe)', offset: 0 },
  { name: 'CET (Central Europe)', offset: 60 },
  { name: 'EET (Eastern Europe)', offset: 120 },
  { name: 'MSK (Moscow)', offset: 180 },
  { name: 'GST (Gulf)', offset: 240 },
  { name: 'IST (India)', offset: 330 },
  { name: 'BST (Bangladesh)', offset: 360 },
  { name: 'ICT (Indochina)', offset: 420 },
  { name: 'CST (China)', offset: 480 },
  { name: 'JST (Japan)', offset: 540 },
  { name: 'AEST (Australia East)', offset: 600 },
  { name: 'ACDT (Australia Central)', offset: 630 },
  { name: 'AEDT (Australia East DST)', offset: 660 },
  { name: 'NZST (New Zealand)', offset: 720 },
  { name: 'AZOT (Azores)', offset: -60 },
  { name: 'CVT (Cape Verde)', offset: -60 },
  { name: 'BRT (Brasilia)', offset: -180 },
  { name: 'ART (Argentina)', offset: -180 },
  { name: 'CLT (Chile)', offset: -240 },
  { name: 'EST (Eastern US)', offset: -300 },
  { name: 'CST (Central US)', offset: -360 },
  { name: 'MST (Mountain US)', offset: -420 },
  { name: 'PST (Pacific US)', offset: -480 },
  { name: 'AKST (Alaska)', offset: -540 },
  { name: 'HST (Hawaii)', offset: -600 },
  { name: 'SST (Samoa)', offset: -660 }
];

function populateTZ() {
  const fromSel = document.getElementById('tzFrom') as HTMLSelectElement;
  const toSel = document.getElementById('tzTo') as HTMLSelectElement;
  fromSel.innerHTML = '';
  toSel.innerHTML = '';
  for (const tz of timeZones) {
    const opt = '<option value="' + tz.offset + '">' + tz.name + '</option>';
    fromSel.innerHTML += opt;
    toSel.innerHTML += opt;
  }
  fromSel.value = '0';
  toSel.value = '-300';
}

const MONTHS = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May', 'Jun', 'Jul', 'Aug', 'Sep', 'Oct', 'Nov', 'Dec'];

function fmtTime(d: Date): string {
  const h = d.getUTCHours();
  const m = d.getUTCMinutes();
  const ampm = h >= 12 ? 'PM' : 'AM';
  return (h % 12 || 12) + ':' + String(m).padStart(2, '0') + ' ' + ampm;
}

function fmtDate(d: Date): string {
  return MONTHS[d.getUTCMonth()] + ' ' + d.getUTCDate() + ', ' + d.getUTCFullYear();
}

function convertTZ() {
  const fromOffset = parseInt((document.getElementById('tzFrom') as HTMLSelectElement).value);
  const toOffset = parseInt((document.getElementById('tzTo') as HTMLSelectElement).value);
  const dateVal = (document.getElementById('tzDate') as HTMLInputElement).value;
  const timeVal = (document.getElementById('tzTime') as HTMLInputElement).value;

  if (!dateVal || !timeVal) return;

  const parts = dateVal.split('-');
  const timeParts = timeVal.split(':');
  const sourceDate = new Date(Date.UTC(parseInt(parts[0]), parseInt(parts[1]) - 1, parseInt(parts[2]), parseInt(timeParts[0]), parseInt(timeParts[1])));

  const utcMs = sourceDate.getTime() - (fromOffset * 60000);
  const targetDate = new Date(utcMs + (toOffset * 60000));

  const sourceTime = fmtTime(sourceDate);
  const sourceDateStr = fmtDate(sourceDate);
  const targetTime = fmtTime(targetDate);
  const targetDateStr = fmtDate(targetDate);

  const diffMin = toOffset - fromOffset;
  const diffHours = Math.floor(Math.abs(diffMin) / 60);
  const diffMins = Math.abs(diffMin) % 60;
  const diffStr = (diffMin >= 0 ? '+' : '-') + diffHours + (diffMins ? ':' + String(diffMins).padStart(2, '0') : '') + ' hours';

  document.getElementById('tzResultValue')!.textContent = targetTime;
  document.getElementById('tzResultLabel')!.textContent = targetDateStr;
  document.getElementById('tzSourceDisplay')!.textContent = sourceDateStr + ' ' + sourceTime;
  document.getElementById('tzTargetDisplay')!.textContent = targetDateStr + ' ' + targetTime;
  document.getElementById('tzDiff')!.textContent = diffStr;
  showResult('tzResult');
}

function resetTZ() {
  const now = new Date();
  (document.getElementById('tzDate') as HTMLInputElement).value = now.toISOString().split('T')[0];
  (document.getElementById('tzTime') as HTMLInputElement).value = now.toTimeString().slice(0, 5);
  const localOffset = -now.getTimezoneOffset();
  (document.getElementById('tzFrom') as HTMLSelectElement).value = String(localOffset);
  convertTZ();
}

document.getElementById('convertBtn')!.addEventListener('click', convertTZ);
document.getElementById('nowBtn')!.addEventListener('click', resetTZ);
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => { populateTZ(); resetTZ(); });
</script>
